# 0024 — Ubuntu's AppArmor userns-restriction SAUCE, forward-ported to 7.2.
# NOT ours: authored by John Johansen in Ubuntu's tree, extracted by diffing
# their 7.0.0-30.30 security/apparmor against vanilla v7.0 and keeping only
# the userns hunks. Same Track B carry as 0022 and 0023 — his authorship
# stays, we add no Signed-off-by.
#
# Why it exists: hyphaed builds from kernel.org, so it does not get SAUCE.
# Vanilla 7.2 already has the whole mediation MECHANISM — the userns_create
# LSM hook, apparmor_userns_create(), aa_profile_ns_perm(), AA_USERNS_CREATE
# and its audit path — and even the upstreamed sibling knob
# apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined. What never went upstream is the
# switch. So a stock build returns 0 for any unconfined task, which is nearly
# everything on a desktop, and Ubuntu's own
# /usr/lib/sysctl.d/10-apparmor.conf fails every boot trying to set a sysctl
# that does not exist. The machine ends up LESS confined than the -generic
# kernel next to it in GRUB.
#
# It transitions rather than denies, and that distinction is the patch. A
# deny-only version would have been a fifth the code and would have broken
# Chrome, bwrap, flatpak and podman, because Ubuntu's policy ships an
# unprivileged_userns profile precisely so those get confined-and-allowed
# instead of refused. Both /etc/apparmor.d/unprivileged_userns and
# bwrap-userns-restrict are installed on this machine.
#
# Compiled clean with W=1; checkpatch 0 errors, 1 warning (Ubuntu's own
# 115-char audit string, left on one line so log messages stay greppable).
#
# NOT BOOTED. This is a mediation path, so a wrong transition degrades
# confinement QUIETLY rather than failing loudly. Before trusting it:
#   sysctl kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns   # exists, = 1
#   unshare -Ur true                                      # confined, not denied
#   aa-status | grep unprivileged_userns
#   then actually start chromium, a flatpak, and a podman container
../custom/0024-apparmor-restrict-unprivileged-userns.patch

# 0028 — small addendum to 0024, OURS (not Johansen's), found on the
# 7.2.0-hyphaed boot audit (2026-08-27). 0024 wired the real userns-create
# mediation but never added the "userns" boolean to
# aa_sfs_entry_unconfined[] in apparmorfs.c — the securityfs advertisement
# Ubuntu's own apparmor.service checks before trusting the sysctl. Missing
# node -> apparmor.service concludes the kernel doesn't support the
